===== app-terminal-panes-ui =====
{
  "subsystemId": "app-terminal-panes-ui",
  "verdict": "recommend",
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "evidence": "TerminalTabStrip.swift and WorkroomTabBar.swift each independently own an identical drag-to-reorder state triple \u2014 `@State private var draggingID: <ID>?`, `dragTranslation: CGFloat = 0`, `widths: [<ID>: CGFloat] = [:]` \u2014 plus near-identical gesture choreography built on top of it (DragGesture .onChanged sets draggingID/dragTranslation and computes a chip-into-pane preview; .onEnded checks a drop target, else calls a local `commitDrag(translation:)` that recomputes the drop index and resets state inside `withAnimation(.easeInOut(duration: 0.2))`). This isn't just parallel structure \u2014 it has already caused a real synchronization failure: both files independently discovered and fixed the SAME bug (committing off the stale `dragTranslation` @State, written only by `onChanged`, instead of the gesture's own final `value.translation`, which a fast/coarse drag can under-shoot), and each fix's comment now points at the other as documentation of the duplication (\"See `WorkroomTabBar`'s identical fix\" / \"...before `onEnded` fires with the full, accurate displacement\"). Two separate PreferenceKeys (`TabWidthKey`, `WorkroomTabWidthKey`) also differ only in their key type. The pure geometry this state feeds (`TabReorder.dropTargetIndex`/`gapShift`, `TabStripOverflow`, `TabStripSplitRun`) is already correctly shared via `TabReorderMath.swift` and unit-tested in `TabReorderMathTests.swift` \u2014 it's specifically the *stateful choreography around* that math which drifted into two hand-synchronized copies.",
      "currentComplexity": "Two `View`s each hold 3 private `@State` vars (draggingID/dragTranslation/widths) plus ~30-40 lines of near-duplicate DragGesture .onChanged/.onEnded/commitDrag logic wired to those vars, kept in sync only by cross-referencing code comments \u2014 the mechanism that already let the same bug ship twice before being independently patched twice.",
      "proposedRepresentation": "A single generic value type (e.g. `TabReorderDrag<ID: Hashable>`, added to TabReorderMath.swift alongside the math it wraps) holding `id: ID?`, `translation: CGFloat`, `widths: [ID: CGFloat]`, with methods `begin(_:)`, `update(translation:)`, `dropIndex(ids:spacing:suspendedByPaneDrag:) -> Int?`, and `commit(ids:spacing:finalTranslation:) -> (from: Int, to: Int)?` that resets state before returning \u2014 so \"always commit off the gesture's own final translation, never the live @State\" is encoded exactly once instead of relied on via comment. Each strip keeps one `@State private var drag = TabReorderDrag<ID>()` and supplies its own commit action (`sessions.moveTab` vs the `workroomTabOrder` array rewrite) and its own drop-into-pane action as call-site closures \u2014 mirroring the division of labor `TabReorder` already uses successfully, so `WorkroomTabBar`'s extra reorder-clamping (`clampReorder`, absent in `TerminalTabStrip`) stays a call-site transform rather than a flag on the shared type. Fold `TabWidthKey`/`WorkroomTabWidthKey` into one generic `PreferenceKey` parameterized the same way.",
      "implementationScope": "Two files: TerminalTabStrip.swift and WorkroomTabBar.swift, plus the new shared type (natural home: TabReorderMath.swift, which already hosts the math it wraps). Verified RootView.swift never touches `draggingID`/`dragTranslation`/`widths` \u2014 it only threads the already-shared `chipPaneDrag`/`localize`/`dropTarget` bindings \u2014 so the blast radius stops at these two view files and does not reach RootView or any other call site.",
      "regressionRisks": "The clamping asymmetry (WorkroomTabBar bounds the drag to the tab run; TerminalTabStrip does not) must land as a call-site translation transform, not a mode flag on the shared type \u2014 baking it into the type would be the wrong seam. Drag-reorder in this codebase has a documented history of real-mouse-only failure modes that synthetic/XCUITest drags don't reproduce (the exact under-shoot bug this recommendation is built on was only caught by comment-driven manual review, not a test), so a refactor here carries real regression risk if verified only by existing automated coverage.",
      "validationNeeded": "Existing `TabReorderMathTests.swift` coverage of `TabReorder`/`TabStripOverflow`/`TabStripSplitRun` must keep passing unchanged (this refactor doesn't touch that pure math). Add a new unit test on the shared type pinning the specific bug this recommendation is grounded in: `commit` must use the gesture's supplied final translation rather than a stale `update`-written value, and must reset `id`/`translation`/`widths` after returning. Because the underlying fault is real-mouse-only, also do a manual drag-reorder pass (fast/coarse flick, not just a slow synthetic drag) in both the terminal tab strip and the workroom tab bar before landing.",
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ]
}

